   "text": "This SMILES was read from the drawing atom by atom, and the stereo flags were worked out from the wedge and hash geometry. The molecule is a lipid A-type disaccharide of two glucosamine-like rings joined by a CH2\u2013O\u2013C1 linkage.\n\n**Left (non-reducing) ring**\n- It has a CH2OH on C5 and a phosphate (OP(=O)(O)O) on C4.\n- C3 carries an O-acyl group, (R)-3-(tetradecanoyloxy)tetradecanoyl.\n- C2 carries an N-acyl group of the same type.\n- C1 is the anomeric carbon, linked through O to the CH2 of the right ring.\n\n**Right (reducing) ring**\n- Going round from the ring oxygen, the ring carbons carry:\n  - OH on C1 (hashed).\n  - OH on C2 (hashed).\n  - NH-acyl on C3, no stereo drawn.\n  - O-acyl on C4 (wedged).\n  - The CH2\u2013O link on C5.\n- The N-acyl and O-acyl groups are both 3-hydroxytetradecanoyl, each with a hashed OH at C3.\n\nI followed the drawing for the right ring even though it differs from natural lipid A, which has the amine at C2 and a free OH at C4. The wedge at C5 of the right ring is read as starting at the ring carbon and pointing to the CH2. This is the one place where the drawing is ambiguous.\n\nChain lengths are taken as tetradecanoyl (14 C) for the ester acyl groups and 3-hydroxytetradecanoyl for the rest. These counts come from reading the drawing and assuming standard lipid A chains.\n\nSMILES: OC[C@H]1O[C@@H](OC[C@H]2O[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C(NC(=O)C[C@H](O)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@H]2OC(=O)C[C@H](O)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@H](NC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@@H](OC(=O)C[C@H](OC(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCC)CCCCCCCCCCC)[C@H]1OP(=O)(O)O"
